[('Piles','Bowel-habit measures and medicines can help some patients; persistent or more advanced disease may need a procedure.'),('Anal fissure','A tear in the anal lining may improve with stool-softening measures and prescribed treatment. An operation may be considered when appropriate.'),('Anal fistula','A tunnel near the anus often needs surgery. Its relationship to the muscles controlling stool affects the treatment choice.')],
'Recovery and wound care differ considerably between procedures. Discuss pain relief, bowel movements, dressings, time away from work and follow-up before surgery.',
'Discuss bleeding, infection, recurrence and any risk to bowel control. Protecting the anal sphincter muscles is particularly important in fistula surgery.',
'Heavy or ongoing bleeding, faintness, or a painful swelling with fever needs urgent medical assessment.',
[('Haemorrhoidectomy for piles','An operation to remove troublesome haemorrhoids. The grade and symptoms of the piles guide whether this is appropriate.'),('Sphincterotomy for anal fissure','A selected portion of the internal anal muscle is divided to reduce tightness and help a chronic fissure heal. The risk to bowel control needs an individual discussion.'),('Fistulotomy, fistulectomy and staged care','Fistulotomy opens the tract; fistulectomy removes it. A seton or a sphincter-preserving approach may be discussed for fistulas involving more muscle. An advancement flap covers the inner opening with nearby tissue.'),('Rectal prolapse and laparoscopic rectopexy','Rectal prolapse is when the rectum slips down and can protrude outside. Rectopexy supports it from inside the abdomen; it is different from piles surgery.'),('Pilonidal sinus','A tract near the top of the buttock cleft can cause swelling or discharge. Assessment determines whether drainage or planned sinus surgery is needed.')],
